// Houdini Atlas — SOP Data Foundations starter pack
// Three small VEX snippets. Each maps to one Attribute Wrangle in the
// node plan. Class is the Wrangle's "Run over" mode.
//
// These snippets are deliberately simple. They exist so the proof you
// produce on the Geometry Spreadsheet has unambiguous, verifiable rows.
//
// === SNIPPET: vex_height_field ===
// Wrangle name: wr_height
// Run over: Points
// Purpose: write a low-frequency height attribute every point can see on the spreadsheet.

vector p = @P;
float n = noise(p * 0.35);          // unit-ish low-frequency noise
@height = fit01(n, -1.0, 1.0);      // explicit range so spreadsheet rows are predictable
// EXPECTED ON SPREADSHEET (Points tab):
//   - new column @height appears
//   - values inside [-1.0, 1.0]
//   - approximately gaussian-shaped distribution

// === SNIPPET: vex_color_from_height ===
// Wrangle name: wr_color
// Run over: Points
// Purpose: encode @height onto @Cd so the viewport tells the truth about the data,
// not a separate visual signal.

float h01 = fit(@height, -1.0, 1.0, 0.0, 1.0);
@Cd = set(h01, h01 * 0.6, 1.0 - h01); // simple cool-warm ramp
// EXPECTED ON SPREADSHEET:
//   - @Cd column written, three components
//   - viewport color matches the spreadsheet column on every point

// === SNIPPET: vex_stable_id (optional, for downstream modules) ===
// Wrangle name: wr_stable_id (insert before any point deletion)
// Run over: Points
// Purpose: capture a stable id so downstream nodes (Solver, Copy to Points)
// can refer to "the same point" across frames or after deletion.

if (!hasattrib(0, "point", "id")) {
    i@id = @ptnum;     // freeze the current ptnum into a stable id
}
// HONESTY: this only works if you wire it BEFORE any node that deletes or
// reorders points. If you add it later, the ids you froze are already different.
